# A causal set, the discrete spacetime of one approach to quantum gravity, machine-checked by the kernel. A causal set
# (causet) replaces the smooth spacetime manifold of general relativity with a SET OF EVENTS carrying a single relation,
# the causal order: x PRECEDES y means x lies in the causal past of y, that x can send influence forward to y. The whole
# of spacetime, both its geometry and its causal structure, is meant to be recovered from this bare order plus a count of
# the events. That is the slogan of the program, ORDER PLUS NUMBER EQUALS GEOMETRY: the partial order fixes the conformal
# (light-cone) structure, the angles and the causal shape, while the NUMBER of events fixes the scale, the spacetime
# volume. Counting events in a region recovers its volume, the discreteness that the continuum hides.
#
# The order is a partial order with three laws, each carrying a physical meaning. It is REFLEXIVE in the convention used
# here, every event precedes itself, the trivial self-influence. It is TRANSITIVE, if x precedes y and y precedes z then
# x precedes z, the composition of causal influence along a chain. It is ANTISYMMETRIC, if x precedes y and y precedes x
# then x and y are the same event, which forbids closed causal loops: there are no causal paradoxes, no event in its own
# strict past. Two events neither of which precedes the other are SPACELIKE, causally unrelated, an ANTICHAIN, and this is
# the discrete image of events outside each other's light cones.
#
# The fourth law is LOCAL FINITENESS, the discreteness itself: between any two causally related events there are only
# FINITELY many events. The set of events causally between two given ones is the ORDER INTERVAL, and its size is finite.
# This is what lets the count stand in for a volume, and it is the property that distinguishes a causal set from a merely
# continuous partial order like the real line.
#
# This file models the smallest nontrivial causet, a CAUSAL DIAMOND: a bottom event, two middle events left and right,
# and a top event. The bottom precedes everything, the top is preceded by everything, and the two middle events each lie
# between bottom and top but are SPACELIKE to each other (neither precedes the other). The order is a finite table, so
# every law below is discharged by the kernel: reflexivity and antisymmetry and transitivity by exhaustive structural
# induction over the events, the spacelike antichain by direct computation, and local finiteness by counting the interval
# between bottom and top and proving it is exactly the finite set {left, right}, of size two.

load @term/seed/code/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# THE EVENTS of the causal diamond. The bottom is the past tip of the diamond, the top its future tip, and left and right
# are the two spacelike-separated events on its waist. Four events, the smallest causet that has a genuine antichain.
form event
  case bottom
  case left
  case right
  case top

# Logical negation on the truth flag, used to phrase the spacelike condition (NOT preceding).
task not
  take a, like flag
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case yes
      send back
        make no
    case no
      send back
        make yes

# Material implication on flags, used to state the conditional order laws (transitivity, antisymmetry) as closed
# computable facts: `then a b` is no only when the antecedent a holds and the consequent b fails.
task then
  take a, like flag
  take b, like flag
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case yes
      send back
        read b
    case no
      send back
        make yes

# Event equality as a flag, the diagonal of the order. Two events are the same exactly when they are the same case.
task is-same
  take x, like event
  take y, like event
  like flag
  fork case, read x
    case bottom
      fork case, read y
        case bottom
          send back
            make yes
        case left
          send back
            make no
        case right
          send back
            make no
        case top
          send back
            make no
    case left
      fork case, read y
        case left
          send back
            make yes
        case bottom
          send back
            make no
        case right
          send back
            make no
        case top
          send back
            make no
    case right
      fork case, read y
        case right
          send back
            make yes
        case bottom
          send back
            make no
        case left
          send back
            make no
        case top
          send back
            make no
    case top
      fork case, read y
        case top
          send back
            make yes
        case bottom
          send back
            make no
        case left
          send back
            make no
        case right
          send back
            make no

# THE CAUSAL ORDER, the one relation of the causet, given as a finite flag table. `precedes x y` is yes when x is in the
# causal past of y. The bottom precedes every event (it is the past tip). Left precedes only itself and the top. Right
# precedes only itself and the top. The top precedes only itself (it is the future tip). Crucially `precedes left right`
# and `precedes right left` are both NO: the two middle events are spacelike, an antichain. Reflexivity is built in, every
# event precedes itself.
task precedes
  take x, like event
  take y, like event
  like flag
  fork case, read x
    case bottom
      send back
        make yes
    case left
      fork case, read y
        case left
          send back
            make yes
        case top
          send back
            make yes
        case bottom
          send back
            make no
        case right
          send back
            make no
    case right
      fork case, read y
        case right
          send back
            make yes
        case top
          send back
            make yes
        case bottom
          send back
            make no
        case left
          send back
            make no
    case top
      fork case, read y
        case top
          send back
            make yes
        case bottom
          send back
            make no
        case left
          send back
            make no
        case right
          send back
            make no

# THE ORDER-INTERVAL MEMBERSHIP, `is-link a x b` (the event x is a LINK of the causal chain from a to b): x lies STRICTLY
# between a and b, the open order interval. It holds when a precedes x and x precedes b, and x is distinct from both ends.
# The set of such x for fixed a and b is the order interval, and local finiteness is the statement that it is always
# finite. In the diamond the interval from bottom to top is exactly {left, right}.
task is-link
  take a, like event
  take x, like event
  take b, like event
  like flag
  send back
    call both
      call both
        call precedes
          read a
          read x
        call precedes
          read x
          read b
      call both
        call not
          call is-same
            read x
            read a
        call not
          call is-same
            read x
            read b

# A TALLY, a finite count built as zero and successor. Carrying the interval size as a tally (rather than a builtin
# integer) keeps the count inside the structural fragment the kernel reduces, so the local-finiteness laws below discharge
# by exhaustive reduction. The tally is exactly the natural-number count of events, the NUMBER of order-plus-number.
form tally
  case none
  case more
    link prior, like tally

# Addition of tallies, by structural recursion on the first: it is the sum of two finite counts. This is how the per-event
# contributions are summed into the size of the order interval.
task add-tally
  take a, like tally
  take b, like tally
  like tally
  fork case, read a
    case none
      send back
        read b
    case more
      link prior
      send back
        make more
          bind prior
            call add-tally
              read prior
              read b

# A truth flag turned into a tally: one for yes, zero for no. The bridge from membership in the order interval to the
# running count.
task flag-tally
  take f, like flag
  like tally
  fork case, read f
    case yes
      send back
        make more
          bind prior
            make none
    case no
      send back
        make none

# A single event's contribution to the interval count: a tally of one when it is a strict link of the interval from a to
# b, a tally of zero (none) otherwise.
task link-count
  take a, like event
  take x, like event
  take b, like event
  like tally
  send back
    call flag-tally
      call is-link
        read a
        read x
        read b

# THE INTERVAL SIZE, `between-count a b`: how many events lie strictly between a and b, the cardinality of the open order
# interval, as a finite tally. It sums the per-event link count over all four events of the causet. Local finiteness is
# the fact that this count is always a finite number, and for the diamond's bottom-to-top interval it is exactly two.
task between-count
  take a, like event
  take b, like event
  like tally
  send back
    call add-tally
      call add-tally
        call link-count
          read a
          make bottom
          read b
        call link-count
          read a
          make left
          read b
      call add-tally
        call link-count
          read a
          make right
          read b
        call link-count
          read a
          make top
          read b

# PRECEDENCE IS REFLEXIVE: precedes e e = yes, for every event e. Every event lies in its own causal past in the reflexive
# convention, the trivial self-influence and the diagonal of the order. Proved by structural induction over the events.
rule precedes-is-reflexive
  mark e, like event
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call precedes
        read e
        read e
      make yes
  fold e

# PRECEDENCE IS TRANSITIVE: if x precedes y and y precedes z then x precedes z, for all events x, y, z. Causal influence
# composes along a chain. Stated as the closed flag fact then(both(precedes x y)(precedes y z))(precedes x z) = yes, and
# discharged by exhaustive structural induction over all four-by-four-by-four event triples.
rule precedes-is-transitive
  mark x, like event
  mark y, like event
  mark z, like event
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call then
        call both
          call precedes
            read x
            read y
          call precedes
            read y
            read z
        call precedes
          read x
          read z
      make yes
  fold x, y, z

# PRECEDENCE IS ANTISYMMETRIC: if x precedes y and y precedes x then x and y are the same event, for all events x, y. This
# is the no-causal-loop law, the statement that the causet has no closed causal curves and no event strictly in its own
# past. Stated as then(both(precedes x y)(precedes y x))(is-same x y) = yes and proved by induction over all event pairs.
rule precedes-is-antisymmetric
  mark x, like event
  mark y, like event
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call then
        call both
          call precedes
            read x
            read y
          call precedes
            read y
            read x
        call is-same
          read x
          read y
      make yes
  fold x, y

# THE MIDDLE EVENTS ARE SPACELIKE: left does not precede right. The two events on the waist of the diamond are causally
# unrelated, outside each other's light cones, the discrete image of spacelike separation. Proved by direct computation.
rule left-does-not-precede-right
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call precedes
        make left
        make right
      make no
  calm hold

# And symmetrically, right does not precede left. Together with the line above, left and right form a two-element
# ANTICHAIN, the simplest nontrivial antichain, the set of mutually spacelike events.
rule right-does-not-precede-left
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call precedes
        make right
        make left
      make no
  calm hold

# THE BOTTOM PRECEDES THE TOP: the past tip of the diamond is in the causal past of the future tip, so the bottom-to-top
# order interval is a genuine causal interval whose finite content the next laws count.
rule bottom-precedes-top
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call precedes
        make bottom
        make top
      make yes
  calm hold

# LEFT LIES IN THE INTERVAL FROM BOTTOM TO TOP: left is a strict link of the causal chain from bottom to top. It is one of
# the two events of the order interval, on its waist.
rule left-is-between-bottom-and-top
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-link
        make bottom
        make left
        make top
      make yes
  calm hold

# RIGHT LIES IN THE INTERVAL FROM BOTTOM TO TOP: right is the other strict link of the chain from bottom to top, the
# second event of the order interval.
rule right-is-between-bottom-and-top
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-link
        make bottom
        make right
        make top
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE ENDPOINTS ARE NOT IN THE OPEN INTERVAL: the bottom is not strictly between bottom and top, since the open interval
# excludes its own ends. This confirms the interval is the OPEN one, the events strictly inside the causal diamond.
rule bottom-is-not-strictly-between-bottom-and-top
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-link
        make bottom
        make bottom
        make top
      make no
  calm hold

# And the top is likewise excluded from the open interval between bottom and top.
rule top-is-not-strictly-between-bottom-and-top
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-link
        make bottom
        make top
        make top
      make no
  calm hold

# LOCAL FINITENESS, THE DISCRETENESS LAW: the order interval between the bottom and the top contains exactly TWO events,
# the finite set {left, right}. The kernel sums the per-event link counts over all four events and reduces the builtin
# integers to two. A FINITE number of events between two related ones, the property that lets this count stand in for a
# spacetime volume. This is the discreteness that recovers geometry from order and number.
rule bottom-to-top-interval-has-two-events
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call between-count
        make bottom
        make top
      make more
        bind prior
          make more
            bind prior
              make none
  calm hold

# THE WAIST IS AN ANTICHAIN WITH AN EMPTY INTERVAL: there are zero events strictly between left and right. They are
# spacelike, neither precedes the other, so the order interval between them is empty, count zero. An empty interval is the
# signature of a causally unrelated pair, the finite (here, vanishing) content of an antichain.
rule left-to-right-interval-is-empty
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call between-count
        make left
        make right
      make none
  calm hold

# THE INTERVAL FROM AN EVENT TO ITSELF IS EMPTY: the open order interval from the bottom to the bottom has zero events,
# since the strict-between relation excludes the endpoints. A degenerate interval has no interior, count zero, the small
# base case of local finiteness.
rule bottom-to-bottom-interval-is-empty
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call between-count
        make bottom
        make bottom
      make none
  calm hold
